From 0743ac8e1536f8314bc586ebef746a86e84ae1b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 10:12:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] odb: let a user's ODB_CODEC reformat the .odb files OpenROAD
 writes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A .odb writes each table's slots one after another, and a slot is a row
of fixed-width fields, so neighbouring bytes belong to unrelated fields
and a generic compressor never sees a run longer than one field. Written
column by column the same bytes compress far better. That reformatting
does not belong in OpenROAD: it is a property of how a file is stored
or shipped, not of the database. What OpenROAD alone knows is where the
slots lie, so this change says that and nothing more, and leaves the
reformatting to a tool the user installs.

- dbDatabase::write(file, layout) writes the same database bytes as
  write(file), plus a text description of where each table's slots lie
  in them (dbStreamLayout.h). Only innermost slots are listed: a chip's
  or a block's slot holds tables of its own.
- With ODB_CODEC set, write_db writes <file>.layout next to the file,
  runs "$ODB_CODEC encode <file> <file>.layout" and removes the layout;
  read_db reads the database from the stdout of "$ODB_CODEC decode
  <file>". A codec is handed every file read_db opens, so one it did not
  encode must come out unchanged.
- Unset, or for a .gz file, nothing changes: no layout is written and
  both commands behave exactly as before.

There is no format change, no schema bump and no change to how the
database is read: read_db still reads stock bytes through the same
operator>>, only from a pipe. The pipe keeps the last 64 KiB it
delivered, since dbIStream seeks back over what it buffered and did not
use when a read stops part way; without it, a codec that hands back
something other than a database would abort openroad rather than fail
read_db. The layout and the codec carry no
compatibility promise; the layout describes the one file it was
written with, and OpenROAD never reads it.

Tests: TestDbStreamLayout checks that asking for a layout leaves the
bytes unchanged, that the runs lie inside the file in order and start
at a slot's allocated flag, and that every instance, net, iterm and
bterm is found where the layout says its table's slots are.
odb_codec_roundtrip writes a database through a stand-in codec and has
a second openroad read it back through it, which must give what
write_db writes without one; it also checks that write_db runs the
codec, removes the layout, and writes no layout without ODB_CODEC.
odb_codec_invalid checks that a file that decodes to something other
than a database fails read_db with its usual error. Both are pass/fail
tests, with no golden log.

Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
